1
00:00:00,090 --> 00:00:02,360
An agent can suggest a change.

2
00:00:02,410 --> 00:00:04,720
That should not mean it can authorize it.

3
00:00:04,770 --> 00:00:07,520
Dungeon Q separates those jobs.

4
00:00:07,570 --> 00:00:12,420
In our recorded September 16th experiment, GPT-6 Astra proposed

5
00:00:12,840 --> 00:00:14,690
two bounded commands.

6
00:00:14,930 --> 00:00:18,120
The runtime required independent review.

7
00:00:18,170 --> 00:00:22,561
The original record includes seven checks and no agent approval tool.

8
00:00:22,641 --> 00:00:25,011
Try the free browser simulation.

9
00:00:25,031 --> 00:00:27,791
Start with an unapproved action, blocked.

10
00:00:27,881 --> 00:00:30,171
Now simulate the separate reviewer.

11
00:00:30,201 --> 00:00:34,171
The approved action changes only the selected synthetic session.

12
00:00:34,191 --> 00:00:35,771
Verify the readback.

13
00:00:35,791 --> 00:00:37,741
Alter a copy, rejected.

14
00:00:37,951 --> 00:00:41,361
Replay, the same receipt, not a second effect.

15
00:00:41,391 --> 00:00:44,131
This browser reviewer is simulated.

16
00:00:44,151 --> 00:00:47,265
Self-hosting provides the authenticated boundary.

17
00:00:47,365 --> 00:00:51,675
Version 0.10 adds administrator-bound alerts.

18
00:00:51,745 --> 00:00:54,795
Here is its recorded local mailbox test.

19
00:00:54,855 --> 00:00:59,295
Contact with the synthetic world creates an alert, but never approves a

20
00:00:59,255 --> 00:01:00,235
rotation.

21
00:01:00,305 --> 00:01:05,195
In the separate defense record, the approved rotation passes four readbacks.

22
00:01:05,265 --> 00:01:09,145
This is a synthetic security lab, not production protection.

23
00:01:09,305 --> 00:01:13,462
Try it in your browser or run the complete open source lab.
